<span class="cat-badge">Guide &middot; Trips</span>
<h1>Hanoi to Ha Long by Motorbike: Route Planning | Hanoi Motorbike Guide</h1>
<p>Ha Long is the sea&rsquo;s answer to Hanoi&rsquo;s traffic, a bay of limestone and water at the end of a long, flat corridor east, and the ride to it is a different animal from the mountain trips the guide usually covers. Where the high routes climb and descend, this one runs long and level, and its challenge is not the terrain but the distance, the traffic and the discipline of riding a plain for hours at a stretch. This guide plans the route honestly: the shape of the ride east, the road types a renter will actually meet, what to verify on the day, and the questions that belong to the rental desk. It refuses to invent distances, durations or road conditions, because the road writes those fresh every morning.</p>
<p>The machine for the corridor can come from Motorbike Rental - Nguyen Tu, 112 Nguyen Van Cu in Long Bien, Hanoi, open daily 09:00 to 21:00, where the desk plans long flat rides with renters daily. Start at the <a href="/hanoi-motorbike-trips/">Hanoi motorbike trips</a> hub, study the parent <a href="/hanoi-motorbike-trips/long-distance-motorbike-trip-planning/">long-distance motorbike trip planning</a> guide, see the machines and terms on the <a href="/hanoi-motorbike-rental/">Hanoi motorbike rental</a> page, review the <a href="/motorbike-guides/">motorbike guides</a> for riding and safety, and keep <a href="/renting-a-motorbike-in-vietnam/">renting a motorbike in Vietnam</a> open for the legal layer.</p>
<nav class="toc">
<ol>
<li><a href="#sec-1">What Ha Long Is</a></li>
<li><a href="#sec-2">The Shape Of The Ride East</a></li>
<li><a href="#sec-3">Road Types And Flat-Road Manners</a></li>
